Here are a few ways to format an ISO timestamp to "Nov 23, 2026" in a web app:

## Native JavaScript
```javascript
const isoTimestamp = "2026-11-23T14:30:00Z";
const date = new Date(isoTimestamp);
const formatted = date.toLocaleDateString("en-US", { 
  year: "numeric", 
  month: "short", 
  day: "numeric" 
});
console.log(formatted); // "Nov 23, 2026"
```

## Using date-fns
```javascript
import { format } from "date-fns";

const isoTimestamp = "2026-11-23T14:30:00Z";
const formatted = format(new Date(isoTimestamp), "MMM d, yyyy");
console.log(formatted); // "Nov 23, 2026"
```

## Using Luxon
```javascript
import { DateTime } from "luxon";

const isoTimestamp = "2026-11-23T14:30:00Z";
const formatted = DateTime.fromISO(isoTimestamp)
  .toFormat("MMM d, yyyy");
console.log(formatted); // "Nov 23, 2026"
```

## React component
```jsx
function TimestampDisplay({ isoTimestamp }) {
  const formatted = new Date(isoTimestamp).toLocaleDateString("en-US", { 
    year: "numeric", 
    month: "short", 
    day: "numeric" 
  });

  return <div>{formatted}</div>;
}
```

**Native Date** works for most cases. Use **date-fns** if you need lightweight formatting utilities, or **Luxon** if you need robust timezone handling. The `toLocaleDateString()` method respects the user's browser locale settings automatically.
